A set is a straightforward way to spot the first repeated node. Before moving ahead, check whether the current node is already stored. The first repeated node is the start of the cycle. If the pointer reaches null, the list has no cycle.

```java
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
}
 */

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public ListNode findStartingPoint(ListNode head) {
        Set<ListNode> seen = new HashSet<>();
        ListNode current = head;

        while (current != null) {
            if (seen.contains(current)) {
                return current;
            }
            seen.add(current);
            current = current.next;
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

This also handles an empty list and a list whose last node points to itself. The extra set makes the code easy to follow, but it uses more memory than the two-pointer method.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(n).

This version follows the links recursively and keeps a set of nodes already on the path. If a node appears again, that node is the cycle entry. Node references are stored in the set, so equal values do not cause a false match.

A dry run on `5 -> 7 -> 9 -> 7` visits 5 and 7 and 9, then sees 7 again and returns that same node.

```csharp
/* Definition for singly-linked list.
 public class ListNode {
     public int val;
     public ListNode next;
     public ListNode(int val=0, ListNode next=null) {
         this.val = val;
         this.next = next;
     }
 }
*/

using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public ListNode FindStartingPoint(ListNode head) {
        var visited = new HashSet<ListNode>();
        return Find(head, visited);
    }

    private ListNode Find(ListNode node, HashSet<ListNode> visited) {
        if (node == null) {
            return null;
        }

        if (visited.Contains(node)) {
            return node;
        }

        visited.Add(node);
        return Find(node.next, visited);
    }
}
```

Important edge cases are an empty list, a single node pointing to itself, and a list with no cycle. The recursive calls use stack space, so a very long list may make this less suitable than an iterative version.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(n) for the set and recursion stack.

Floyd's two-pointer method finds the cycle without changing the list. The slow pointer moves one step and the fast pointer moves two. If they meet, moving one pointer back to the head and then moving both one step at a time leads to the cycle entry.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode *findStartingPoint(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode* slow = head;
        ListNode* fast = head;

        while (fast != NULL && fast->next != NULL) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;

            if (slow == fast) {
                slow = head;
                while (slow != fast) {
                    slow = slow->next;
                    fast = fast->next;
                }
                return slow;
            }
        }

        return NULL;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(n), where n is the number of nodes checked.
Space complexity: O(1).

A Go map can store each node pointer as it is visited. Since pointers are comparable in Go, the map records node identity rather than just the node value. This matters when different nodes contain the same value.

```go
/*Definition of singly linked list:
type ListNode struct {
    Val  int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func findStartingPoint(head *ListNode) *ListNode {
    visited := make(map[*ListNode]bool)
    current := head

    for current != nil {
        if visited[current] {
            return current
        }
        visited[current] = true
        current = current.Next
    }

    return nil
}
```

The empty list returns `nil`, and a normal list also returns `nil` after reaching its end. The main trade-off is the map allocation for every node visited.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(n).

